package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Human-centred original serials about courage, community, and discovery. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch11 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries("the_last_seat","The Last Seat","A new student and a quiet classmate change how their school welcomes people.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"A Seat by the Window",listOf("When Daniel transferred to Unity Secondary, the only empty seat was beside the window. He unpacked quietly while the class discussed a group history project. Across the aisle, Mariam noticed that he had no textbook and slid her spare copy onto his desk.","Daniel thanked her, but when the teacher asked everyone to form teams, he stayed still. He had changed schools three times in two years and had learned not to assume invitations would last.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Group List",listOf("Mariam's team had four members already. Their captain, Tobi, said there was no room, even though the assignment allowed five. Mariam asked whether they could make space, but Tobi worried that a new member would slow them down.","Daniel heard the conversation and offered to work alone. Their teacher reminded the class that the task was about learning history, not selecting the fastest group.")),
            StoryPart(3,"A Different Skill",listOf("The team met in the library. Daniel said little until he saw their notes about the old railway station. He recognised the building from a community archive his aunt helped maintain.","He knew how to request photographs, check dates, and distinguish a first-hand account from a rumour. The team realised he had a skill they did not, and Tobi asked him to help verify their sources.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Interview",listOf("Daniel arranged an interview with Mrs Eze, a retired station clerk, after obtaining permission through the archive. She described how the station connected farms, markets, and schools before the road network expanded.","Mariam recorded the interview with Mrs Eze's consent. Tobi prepared questions, while Daniel checked the dates against old timetables. Their project began to sound like a real account rather than a list copied from the internet.")),
            StoryPart(5,"A Disagreement",listOf("The team disagreed about including a photograph that showed several workers. One student wanted to use it because it looked dramatic; Daniel pointed out that they did not know who the people were or whether the image could be shared.","Their teacher helped them contact the archive. The archivist confirmed the photograph's public-use permission and identified the workers from a caption on the original print.")),
            StoryPart(6,"The Empty Chair",listOf("At the next meeting, Tobi did not arrive. Mariam learned that he had been caring for his younger brother after school and had fallen behind on his own work. The team had assumed he was avoiding responsibility.","Daniel suggested dividing the remaining tasks into smaller pieces and checking what Tobi could realistically complete. Tobi returned the next day and apologised for not explaining sooner.")),
            StoryPart(7,"A Better Plan",listOf("The team created a shared schedule with clear responsibilities. Daniel handled source verification, Mariam prepared the interview, Tobi designed the timeline, and the other members created the map and captions.","They also agreed that anyone could ask for help without being mocked. The work improved because everyone knew what was expected and had a fair chance to contribute.")),
            StoryPart(8,"Presentation Day",listOf("Their presentation included the station's history, the workers' names, and Mrs Eze's account. Daniel spoke about how records can disappear when nobody takes responsibility for preserving them.","During questions, a student challenged one date. Daniel calmly showed the timetable and explained why the team had chosen it. Their teacher praised the group for correcting an early mistake rather than hiding it.")),
            StoryPart(9,"The Invitation",listOf("The school history club invited the team to help create a small archive of neighbourhood memories. Daniel hesitated; he did not want to become the person expected to do everything because he had experience.","Mariam suggested a rotating team so the responsibility would be shared. Daniel agreed to join for one term, with a clear schedule and support from the teacher.")),
            StoryPart(10,"Room at the Table",listOf("At the end of term, Daniel had friends he could rely on and a school project he was proud to show his aunt. Tobi had learned to ask questions before making assumptions, and the group had become more organised and considerate.","The class kept the window seat open for visiting students during club meetings. It was not a special seat reserved for one person; it was a reminder that belonging grows when people make room, listen, and share responsibility."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_bridge_of_names","The Bridge of Names","Two young neighbours uncover the people behind the names on an old footbridge.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"Names in the Rail",listOf("Nneka and Samuel crossed the old footbridge on their way to the community centre. Its metal rail carried a row of engraved names, most too faded to read. A new sign announced that the bridge would be renovated, and the pair wondered who the names belonged to.","They asked the centre coordinator, who explained that the bridge had been built by residents decades earlier. The original record book was believed to be somewhere in the council archive.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Archive Request",listOf("With help from their teacher, they submitted a formal request to view the public construction records. The archive staff found a ledger, but several pages had been damaged by moisture.","The surviving entries listed donations of timber, tools, transport, and labour. Some residents had given money; others had cooked meals or carried supplies. The bridge had been a collective project.")),
            StoryPart(3,"A Name Repeated",listOf("One name appeared more often than the others: Alhaji Musa. Samuel assumed he had paid for most of the bridge, but the ledger showed small contributions and repeated notes about organising volunteers.","An elderly neighbour remembered Musa as a careful coordinator who kept lists so every contribution was acknowledged. She said the bridge was never meant to celebrate one wealthy person.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Carpenter's Family",listOf("Nneka found the family of the carpenter whose initials appeared beside the bridge design. His daughter, Mrs Bassey, showed them a sketch with measurements and notes about using materials available locally.","She explained that her father had worked with a team and had insisted that the bridge be checked by a qualified engineer before it opened. The family still had the inspection certificate.")),
            StoryPart(5,"A Necessary Correction",listOf("The certificate revealed that one engraved name had been misspelled. The students told the coordinator, who warned them not to alter the rail themselves. The renovation team would need verified records before making any correction.","They compared the certificate, ledger, and family documents. The evidence supported the correct spelling, but one other name remained uncertain.")),
            StoryPart(6,"The Missing Volunteer",listOf("A local newspaper archive contained a photograph of the opening ceremony. In the background stood a woman holding a notebook, identified only as 'Mrs A.' A retired teacher recognised her as Amina, who had coordinated meals and first aid for the volunteers.","Amina's son confirmed the story and shared a letter thanking her team. He asked that the family be consulted before any new plaque included her name.")),
            StoryPart(7,"A Public Meeting",listOf("The coordinator organised a meeting with families, engineers, and residents. Nneka and Samuel presented the documents and explained which details were confirmed and which still needed research.","Some residents wanted a grand monument. Others preferred a modest plaque listing the whole volunteer team. The engineer reminded everyone that the renovation's first priority was a safe, accessible crossing.")),
            StoryPart(8,"The New Design",listOf("The approved plan included the required structural work and a small weather-resistant history panel beside the entrance. The panel would list verified names and describe the different kinds of contribution without ranking them.","Families reviewed the spelling and wording. A name with insufficient evidence was left off the panel but recorded as a research question in the archive, rather than guessed.")),
            StoryPart(9,"Opening the Crossing",listOf("After the work passed inspection, the bridge reopened. The panel honoured the carpenter, the coordinator, Amina's volunteer team, and the many residents whose names had survived in the ledger.","Samuel noticed that children stopped to read the panel on their way home. He and Nneka placed copies of the source records in the community centre so the history could be checked by anyone.")),
            StoryPart(10,"More Than a Name",listOf("The students donated their research notes to the archive, including a clear list of unresolved questions. The centre began an oral-history project so families could contribute memories with permission.","The bridge carried people across the stream as it always had, but now its history was easier to understand. Nneka and Samuel learned that public memory should be accurate, shared, and open to correction. Every name mattered, and so did the truth behind it."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_sunday_radio","The Sunday Radio","A teenager helps her grandfather preserve a neighbourhood radio programme without losing its purpose.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"Static at Seven",listOf("Every Sunday at seven, Grandpa Bako tuned an old radio to a community programme. One week, the signal dissolved into static. He sighed and said the station might be ending its local broadcasts.","His granddaughter Kemi offered to help find out what had changed. Grandpa warned her that the programme was more than music: it shared verified announcements, interviews, and practical information for residents.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Station Visit",listOf("Kemi and her grandfather visited the station during its public open hours. The producer explained that a transmitter fault had interrupted the broadcast, while rising costs had forced the team to review its schedule.","The programme could return, but the station needed volunteers to help organise community contributions and check announcements before they went on air.")),
            StoryPart(3,"Old Recordings",listOf("The producer played recordings from earlier years. Kemi heard interviews with market traders, teachers, nurses, and bus drivers. Each segment had a date and a consent form attached.","She noticed that some recordings had no clear description. The producer explained that the station was building a proper archive and needed help identifying speakers and confirming which clips could be reused.")),
            StoryPart(4,"A Voice Remembered",listOf("Grandpa recognised a voice from an interview about a flood-preparedness project. It belonged to Mr Lawal, a retired community health worker who had helped organise local information sessions.","Kemi contacted him through the station, and he agreed to review the recording. He corrected one outdated phone number and explained that the advice needed updating before it could be broadcast again.")),
            StoryPart(5,"Checking Before Sharing",listOf("Kemi helped the producer create a checklist: confirm dates, verify contact details, obtain permission, and distinguish personal opinions from official announcements. The team agreed that urgent safety information must come from responsible authorities.","Grandpa liked the checklist. He said a familiar voice could make information feel trustworthy, but trust also required checking the facts.")),
            StoryPart(6,"A New Generation",listOf("The station invited students to propose a short segment called 'Ask the Neighbourhood'. Kemi suggested interviewing people about skills they were willing to teach, such as bicycle repair, reading support, and gardening.","The producer approved a pilot with clear boundaries. Participants could choose what to share, review their quotes, and decline any question. The programme would not publish private addresses or personal contact details.")),
            StoryPart(7,"The First Recording",listOf("Kemi interviewed a tailor who taught apprentices how to repair school uniforms and a librarian who ran a weekly reading circle. Grandpa sat nearby, proud but careful not to answer for the guests.","When Kemi made an editing mistake, the producer showed her how to correct it without changing what the speakers meant. She learned that a good interview was built on listening, not filling every silence.")),
            StoryPart(8,"The Broadcast Plan",listOf("The station confirmed its transmitter repairs and scheduled the programme's return. The team tested the audio, checked the segment timings, and reviewed each participant's permission.","They also prepared a written summary for residents who could not listen live. Kemi insisted that the summary include the broadcast date so nobody would mistake old information for a current announcement.")),
            StoryPart(9,"Sunday Returns",listOf("At seven on Sunday, the familiar programme returned. Grandpa turned the volume up as the host introduced the new segment and thanked the guests by their approved names.","Listeners sent questions through the station's official channel. The producer selected a few for a future episode and reminded the audience that the station could not verify every message immediately.")),
            StoryPart(10,"A Signal That Serves",listOf("Kemi continued volunteering one afternoon each week. The station created an archive index, a consent register, and a schedule for reviewing information before rebroadcasting it. Grandpa donated his old recordings after the team checked their permissions.","The radio's signal had returned, but the bigger achievement was a stronger programme: one that welcomed new voices while protecting accuracy and privacy. Grandpa and Kemi listened together, knowing that a community broadcast mattered most when it served its listeners responsibly."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_red_scarf_project","The Red Scarf Project","A school sewing club turns a forgotten craft into a practical intergenerational project.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"A Box of Wool",listOf("When Ifeoma joined the school sewing club, she found a box of red wool and a note from a former student: 'Make something useful, then teach someone else.' Nobody knew who had written it.","The club planned to make decorations for its exhibition, but Ifeoma suggested making warm scarves for a local shelter. Their teacher said they would first need to ask the shelter what was actually useful.")),
            StoryPart(2,"Ask First",listOf("The shelter coordinator explained that scarves were welcome, but only if they were washable, comfortable, and made in suitable sizes. The shelter also needed plain colours because some residents preferred not to wear bright patterns.","The students adjusted their plan and asked a textile teacher to explain safe materials and basic care labels. They decided to make a small sample before promising a large donation.")),
            StoryPart(3,"An Unexpected Teacher",listOf("A retired tailor named Mrs Nwosu visited the club. She recognised the note's handwriting as belonging to her former apprentice, who had started the sewing club years earlier.","Mrs Nwosu taught the students how to measure, count stitches, and check seams. She reminded them that useful work should be durable, not merely attractive in a photograph.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The First Sample",listOf("The first scarf was too narrow and unravelled at one end. Ifeoma wanted to hide it, but Mrs Nwosu asked the group to inspect the problem together and record what they would change.","They learned to secure the edges and test the fabric after washing. The second sample held its shape, and the shelter coordinator approved the design.")),
            StoryPart(5,"The Story Behind the Note",listOf("Mrs Nwosu explained that the former apprentice, Ayo, had started the club after receiving help from neighbours when his family faced a difficult year. He wanted students to learn a skill and pass it on.","Ayo now lived in another city and worked as a costume designer. Mrs Nwosu suggested inviting him to a video call, but only if he was comfortable joining.")),
            StoryPart(6,"A Shared Lesson",listOf("Ayo joined the club meeting and showed photographs of early projects. He praised the students for asking the shelter what residents needed instead of assuming they knew.","He also explained that the club had once kept a pattern book so future members could repeat successful designs. The book had been misplaced during a classroom move.")),
            StoryPart(7,"Finding the Pattern Book",listOf("The teacher checked the school's storage register and found a box of old club materials. Inside was the pattern book, along with notes about fabric sources and care instructions.","The students digitised the patterns with the school's permission and kept the original safely. They added a date and author credit to each entry where the evidence was clear.")),
            StoryPart(8,"Making Together",listOf("Families and volunteers joined a supervised workshop. Some people knitted, others checked labels, and younger students prepared packaging. Nobody was pressured to contribute money or materials.","The club made only the quantity the shelter had requested. Each item included a simple care label, and the coordinator arranged a suitable delivery time.")),
            StoryPart(9,"A Useful Exhibition",listOf("At the school exhibition, the club displayed its sample scarves, pattern book, and photographs of the process. They explained how feedback had changed the design and why they had avoided making more than the shelter could use.","The shelter sent a note thanking the students and reporting that the scarves were practical for residents. The club decided to ask for feedback again before planning another project.")),
            StoryPart(10,"Pass It On",listOf("The school added the pattern book to its learning archive and scheduled an annual skills workshop with local craftspeople. Ayo agreed to mentor the next club leaders remotely when his schedule allowed.","Ifeoma wrote a new note and placed it in the wool box: 'Make something useful, listen to the people who will use it, and teach the next person.' The red scarf project had become more than a donation. It was a tradition of thoughtful making and shared knowledge."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_hidden_gardeners","The Hidden Gardeners","Residents discover the people quietly caring for a neglected public green space.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"Green Behind the Fence",listOf("Chidi passed the unused lot every day, but one morning he noticed fresh leaves growing behind the fence. A small patch of soil had been cleared, and a hand-painted sign read 'Please leave the seedlings in place.'","He told his friend Binta, who suggested asking the residents' association before entering. The association knew the land was public but had no record of a garden project.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Watering Can",listOf("A caretaker at the nearby clinic said someone watered the lot early each morning. He had seen an older woman carrying a blue watering can, but he did not know her name.","Chidi and Binta asked the association to post a notice inviting the gardener to contact them. They did not want to disturb the plants or publish guesses about who was responsible.")),
            StoryPart(3,"Mrs Danjuma",listOf("The gardener introduced herself as Mrs Danjuma, a retired biology teacher. She had planted hardy local species after noticing that rainwater collected in the lot and left the soil bare elsewhere.","She explained that several neighbours helped, each with permission from the association. They had been keeping the project small until they knew whether the land was scheduled for construction.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Land Record",listOf("The association checked the public land record and contacted the relevant council office. The lot was reserved as a small green space, but the old maintenance agreement had expired.","The residents were advised to submit a simple stewardship proposal, including access, waste collection, water use, and a plan to avoid blocking drainage routes.")),
            StoryPart(5,"A Community Plan",listOf("Chidi helped prepare a map showing the existing plants, walking path, and drainage channel. Binta gathered suggestions from neighbours, including a request for a shaded seat and a clear route for wheelchair users.","Mrs Danjuma reminded them not to remove existing plants until they knew what each one was. A local horticulture officer offered to identify the species and advise on suitable maintenance.")),
            StoryPart(6,"The Plant List",listOf("The officer identified several native plants and recommended adding ground cover to reduce soil erosion. The group removed litter using an organised community clean-up and proper protective equipment supplied by the association.","They recorded every change and kept the drainage channel clear. The garden was becoming healthier, but the residents knew that a single clean-up would not keep it that way.")),
            StoryPart(7,"The Agreement",listOf("The council approved a one-year volunteer stewardship agreement. It set out what the group could maintain, what required professional approval, and how residents could report damage or safety concerns.","The agreement also required a public contact point and quarterly reviews. Mrs Danjuma said the rules were useful because they protected both the garden and the volunteers.")),
            StoryPart(8,"A Place to Meet",listOf("A local carpenter built a simple bench from approved materials, and the association arranged an accessible path. Children made plant labels during a supervised workshop, using names confirmed by the horticulture officer.","The group scheduled watering and maintenance duties so no one person carried the whole responsibility. Neighbours who could not garden helped with records, notices, or occasional checks.")),
            StoryPart(9,"The Open Morning",listOf("The residents held an open morning where Mrs Danjuma explained the garden's plants and the council officer answered questions about the stewardship agreement. Chidi and Binta showed the before-and-after map.","Visitors suggested a composting trial, but the group agreed to research it before starting. They wanted the garden to remain tidy, accessible, and manageable for the people who had volunteered.")),
            StoryPart(10,"A Garden with Many Hands",listOf("At the one-year review, the council renewed the agreement after confirming that the space was well maintained and the drainage remained clear. The garden had become a quiet place to rest, learn, and meet neighbours.","Mrs Danjuma handed the watering schedule to a new volunteer team. Chidi and Binta understood the hidden gardeners were never hidden because they wanted a mystery; they simply preferred useful work to attention. The garden flourished because care had become shared."))
        ))
    )
}
